P1_ADD = [
    "To understand the deep mathematical foundation of Oklch, consider the geometry of human color sensation. The human retina contains three distinct classes of cone photoreceptor cells sensitive to short (S), medium (M), and long (L) wavelengths of light. However, the human brain does not interpret raw LMS signals directly; it processes them through an opponent color channel network: Light-Dark (achromatic luminance), Red-Green opponent signals, and Yellow-Blue opponent signals. The Oklab and Oklch color spaces—developed by optical researcher Björn Ottosson in 2020—directly model this human neural opponent transform using non-linear cube root compression, delivering unprecedented perceptual uniformity.",
    "Furthermore, Oklch eliminates the notorious 'hue shift bug' that plagues legacy color spaces during color interpolation. When you interpolate between blue and white in HSL or sRGB, the color path passes through an unwanted, muddy purple tint. In contrast, interpolating in Oklch follows a straight, perceptually pure trajectory through color space, maintaining absolute hue stability. This mathematical purity ensures that hover states, gradient fills, and data visualization heatmaps render with crystal-clear color fidelity without unsightly hue drift."
]

